# Understanding Header-Only Retrieval with cURL in PHP

When building scripts to monitor remote resources, efficiency matters. You might assume that fetching only HTTP headers consumes less bandwidth and processing power than downloading a full payload. In reality, the server's workload is largely identical regardless of whether you request the body or not. The server must parse the content, generate status codes, and compute metadata like `Last-Modified` or `ETag`. The primary benefit of header-only retrieval lies on your side: it drastically reduces network transfer time and memory usage by avoiding the overhead of parsing large response bodies.

However, retrieving that data in PHP with cURL requires careful configuration. A common stumbling block involves how cURL handles headers versus body data internally. If you enable `CURLOPT_HEADER` without disabling the body, or if you mix flags incorrectly, the API can return confusing results.

## Correctly Configuring cURL for Headers Only

To fetch headers exclusively, you must set two critical options: `CURLOPT_NOBODY` and `CURLOPT_HEADER`. Setting `CURLOPT_NOBODY` to `true` instructs cURL to download nothing but the header. This prevents the transfer of the actual content payload. You should also ensure `CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION` is managed appropriately if you want to avoid redirect loops affecting the header retrieval logic, though often default behavior suffices for simple checks.

```php
$curl = curl_init();

// Fetch headers only
curl_setopt($curl, CURLOPT_NOBODY, true);
curl_setopt($curl, CURLOPT_HEADER, true); // This ensures we can see the output if needed

curl_setopt($curl, CURLOPT_URL, 'http://url/file.xml');

// Execute the request
curl_exec($curl);

// Close the handle
curl_close($curl);
```

In this setup, `curl_exec()` executes the request but returns an empty string because no body was transferred. To inspect the raw headers received by cURL, you must use `curl_getinfo()`. Crucially, when `CURLOPT_NOBODY` is active, the first argument passed to `curl_getinfo()` must be the cURL handle (resource), not the result of `curl_exec()`. Passing the output of `curl_exec()`âwhich is empty in a header-only requestâto `curl_getinfo()` will fail to retrieve metadata.

## Accessing Metadata via curl_getinfo

Once the handle is correctly passed to `curl_getinfo`, you can extract specific headers that indicate file freshness. The most common approach for conditional GET requests is checking the `Last-Modified` header.

```php
$curl = curl_init();
curl_setopt($curl, CURLOPT_NOBODY, true);
curl_setopt($curl, CURLOPT_URL, 'http://example.com/data.xml');
curl_exec($curl);

if (curl_getinfo($curl, CURLINFO_HTTP_CODE) == 200) {
    $lastModified = curl_getinfo($curl, CURLINFO_LAST_MODIFIED);
    echo "Last Modified: " . $lastModified;
}

curl_close($curl);
```

Alternatively, you can access the raw header string directly. When `CURLOPT_HEADER` is set to true, cURL populates the response with headers. However, note that if `CURLOPT_NOBODY` is true, the body part of the response is omitted, which aligns perfectly with your goal of minimizing data transfer.

If you need to implement an `If-Modified-Since` strategy, you must first fetch the `Last-Modified` header from the server using the method above. You can then construct a subsequent request including that timestamp in the headers. If the resource hasn't changed, the server returns a 304 Not Modified, allowing your script to skip re-downloading the file entirely.

## Common Pitfalls in Implementation

One frequent error is misinterpreting the return value of `curl_exec()` when header-only mode is active. As seen in user reports, developers sometimes see headers printed even without explicitly requesting them if they accidentally leave body-fetching logic active or misinterpret the output stream. Always verify that `CURLOPT_NOBODY` is set before relying on an empty `curl_exec()` return value.

Another subtle issue involves mixing `curl_getinfo()` calls with wrong resource types. If you store the result of `curl_exec()` into a variable and pass that variable instead of the handle to `curl_getinfo()`, you will get `NULL` for most metadata fields, including `datetime` or custom header values. This often leads to confusion when trying to access `$this->last_change['datetime']` without realizing the underlying resource was invalid.

By strictly separating the execution of the request (`curl_exec`) from the inspection of metadata (`curl_getinfo`), and ensuring the correct handle is passed, you can reliably build lightweight polling mechanisms that respect server-side caching policies.
